{"id":"120c0c0a-eb4b-48ea-8ce0-ebafdca36f6b","object":"chat.completion","created":1784826476,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = payload['required_symbols']\\n    # Map node id to node\\n    node_map = {n['id']: dict(n) for n in nodes}\\n    # Build children list\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for nid, n in node_map.items():\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        if parent is None:\\n            if root_id is not None:\\n                raise ValueError('Multiple roots')\\n            root_id = nid\\n        else:\\n            if parent not in node_map:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Parent {parent} not found')\\n            children[parent].append(nid)\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root')\\n    # Check all required symbols present initially\\n    symbols_present = set(n['symbol'] for n in node_map.values())\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in symbols_present:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym} missing')\\n    # Precompute subtree nodes for each node (including itself)\\n    # Use DFS post-order\\n    subtree_nodes = {}\\n    def dfs(nid):\\n        nodes_in_sub = {nid}\\n        for cid in children[nid]:\\n            nodes_in_sub.update(dfs(cid))\\n        subtree_nodes[nid] = nodes_in_sub\\n        return nodes_in_sub\\n    dfs(root_id)\\n    # Precompute symbol counts for each subtree\\n    subtree_symbols = {}\\n    for nid, sub in subtree_nodes.items():\\n        counts = {}\\n        for sid in sub:\\n            sym = node_map[sid]['symbol']\\n            counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n        subtree_symbols[nid] = counts\\n    # Total symbol counts\\n    total_counts = subtree_symbols[root_id].copy()\\n    # Process nodes in order: deeper first, then by node id\\n    # Collect all nodes except root\\n    candidates = [nid for nid in node_map if nid != root_id and node_map[nid].get('removable', False)]\\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    # For checking retained children, we need to know if a child is removed.\\n    # Since we process deeper first, we can maintain removed set.\\n    for nid in candidates:\\n        # Check if node is already removed (shouldn't happen)\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if any child is retained (i.e., not removed)\\n        child_retained = False\\n        for cid in children[nid]:\\n            if cid not in removed:\\n                child_retained = True\\n                break\\n        if child_retained:\\n            continue\\n        # Tentatively remove: check if all required symbols remain after removal\\n        # Compute what would be removed: subtree of nid\\n        sub_counts = subtree_symbols[nid]\\n        # Ensure that for each required symbol, after removal count >= 1\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            after_count = total_counts.get(sym, 0) - sub_counts.get(sym, 0)\\n            if after_count < 1:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        # Actually remove\\n        removed.add(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n        # Update total_counts by subtracting subtree counts\\n        for sym, cnt in sub_counts.items():\\n            total_counts[sym] = total_counts.get(sym, 0) - cnt\\n    # Gather retained ids: all nodes not removed\\n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid in node_map if nid not in removed])\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a Python function `solve(case)` that implements grammar-guided node reduction as described. The task: given a tree of nodes with parent, symbol, depth, removable flag, utility, and required_symbols, we need to reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving that all required_symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains). Also, we cannot remove root, or nodes that have retained children (i.e., if a node is removed, its children are gone? Actually, in tree reduction, removing a node means its children are also removed? Or do we replace? The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It also says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That last part is ambiguous: \"node with retained children\" might mean a node that has children that are not removed? Actually, typical reduction: you can remove a node if its subtree is not needed, but if you remove a node, its children are gone. But \"retained children\" might mean children that are being kept? Possibly they mean that if a node has children that are not removed (i.e., they are retained), then you cannot remove the parent because that would remove the children. So we must ensure that if we remove a node, all its descendants are also removed. So we need to decide on a set of nodes to remove such that the remaining tree is connected (since removing a node removes its subtree). The root must remain. Required symbols must be present in the remaining tree.\n\nWe need to follow an order: process deeper nodes before shallower nodes. For nodes at same depth, use node ID as tie break (presumably alphabetical or lexicographic order). We tentatively remove a node if it is removable and if after removal, all required symbols still exist somewhere in the tree (i.e., there is at least one node with that symbol that is not removed). But we also must consider that removing a node might remove symbols that are elsewhere? Actually, removing a node removes its entire subtree. So we need to check that after removal, every required symbol still has at least one node remaining (not just the node itself, but any node with that symbol). Also, we cannot remove root. And \"node with retained children\" likely means we cannot remove a node if it has children that are not removed (i.e., some children are kept). But in our greedy algorithm, we consider nodes in order. When we consider a node, we might have already decided to keep some of its children? Actually, the order is deeper first, so children are considered before their parent. So when we reach a parent, we have already decided about its children. If any child is kept, then the parent must be kept (because removing parent would remove that child). So we should not remove a parent if any child is retained. That aligns with \"node with retained children\".\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build a tree structure: each node with id, parent id, children list.\n- Identify nodes that are removable (removable=True) and not root.\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then by node id lexicographically (since IDs are strings). For each node in that order:\n  - Check if it is removable, and if it has any children that are currently retained (i.e., not removed). Since we process deeper first, we can maintain a set of removed nodes. Initially empty.\n  - If node is removable and all its children are removed (or it has no children), then consider tentative removal.\n  - Tentatively remove: simulate removal by checking if all required symbols are still present in the tree after removing this node and its subtree (which is already removed if children are removed). Actually, if we remove this node, its entire subtree is removed. Since we already decided on children, if any child is retained, we cannot remove this node. So the condition \"all children are removed\" already ensures that no child is retained. So we only need to check symbols: after removal, does each required symbol still exist somewhere? We need to count occurrences of symbols in the tree excluding the subtree rooted at this node. Since we haven't actually removed yet, we can compute the current counts of each symbol across all nodes (including this node and its descendants). Then check if after subtracting the counts for this subtree, all required symbols have count >=1.\n  - Also, the node itself is removable and not root.\n  - If conditions satisfied, add node id to removed set. Also record the removal order.\n- After processing all nodes, the retained set is all nodes not in removed set. Then sort retained IDs (presumably by insertion order? The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained IDs should be sorted (likely lexicographically). Removal order is the order in which nodes were removed (the sequence of IDs).\n- Also need to handle infeasible cases: if after reduction, a required symbol is missing (should not happen if algorithm ensures), but initial state must have all required symbols. If not, raise ValueError. Also if structural issues (e.g., parent missing, cycle) raise ValueError. But cases appear valid.\n\nReturn a dict with keys: \"retained_ids\" (list of strings), \"removal_order\" (list of strings). Also maybe \"utility_total\"? The problem says \"return JSON-serializable object\" and \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" Probably just those two fields. But the description also mentions \"compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions\" but that might be for higher-level. For solve, we just return the result.\n\nImplement function solve(case) that takes a dict with payload containing nodes list and required_symbols list. Build node lookup dict. Check that root exists (parent None). Build children mapping. Validate that each node has id, parent (except root), symbol, depth, removable, utility. Ensure all required_symbols are present in initial tree. Then proceed with algorithm.\n\nWe must not mutate case. So copy nodes as needed.\n\nEdge cases: multiple nodes with same symbol? That's fine.\n\nImplement in pure Python.\n\nNow produce JSON with key \"implementation\" and the Python source code as a string, properly escaped.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1849,"completion_tokens":2233,"total_tokens":4082,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1299},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":57},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}